  "source": {
    "title": "Lurgan Mail, 12 June 1920, p. 6 (\"The Elections: County Council\"); labels from the Frontier Sentinel, 12 June 1920, p. 5",
    "publisher": "Lurgan Mail (via the British Newspaper Archive)",
    "kind": "news",
    "check": "first preferences add to the printed valid poll; quota = valid / (seats + 1) + 1 as printed; each surplus equals the elected candidate's total less the quota and equals the printed transfers (plus papers with no further preference); each count is the previous count plus the transfers; every figure read from the page images"
  },
  "note": "Proportional representation (single transferable vote) under the Local Government (Ireland) Act 1919; polled Saturday 5 June 1920 and counted at Armagh by T. E. Reid, secretary of the county council and returning officer. Lurgan electoral area (four seats): the electoral divisions of Brownlowsderry, Carrowbrack, Cornakinnegar, Kernan, Lurgan Rural and Montiaghs and the urban district of Lurgan; polled in the Town Hall and Windsor Avenue school in Lurgan and at eight rural stations. 5,100 valid votes; quota 1,021. Johnston's surplus of 1,255 elected MacGeagh; Mahaffy, the only member of the old council standing in the area, was defeated. Labels differ between papers: the Northern Whig (9 June 1920, p. 7) prints Campbell and Deeny as N., the Armagh Guardian (11 June 1920, p. 3) Campbell as Separatist and Deeny as I.; its first-count figure for Mahaffy (168) is his share of the transfer. David Pedlow withdrew."
